# mm.fs.conformance

The mm.fs contract as checks that run against whatever volume is mounted at a
prefix: the dispositions, append, short reads, extension by seeking past the
end, truncate, sync, stat, access refusals, directory listing and its
NameTooLong retry, make_directory, remove, rename, the Busy rules for open
files, and paths through "..". A host test runs it over an in-memory volume,
and the same checks are meant for every driver and for a target smoke
application, which is what "the same API everywhere" means in practice.

It works inside a scratch directory it makes beneath the prefix and removes
afterwards, and it touches nothing else. What the contract leaves to the
volume -- case sensitivity, timestamps, the order of directory entries --
it does not check. CrossVolume needs two mounts and is mm.fs's own rule, so
mm.fs's tests check it instead.
